</style><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/Shell.BG-gLcGo.css"></head><body id="page-top" class="utk destination-layout"><a href="#main" class="skip">Skip to content</a><div class="topline" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><span data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>ROOTED IN ODISHA. OPEN TO THE WORLD.</span><span lang="or" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>ପ୍ରୋଜେକ୍ଟ ଓଡ଼ିଶା</span></div><header class="header wrap utk-header" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><a class="home-link" href="/" aria-label="Project Odisha home" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><span class="brand brand-en" lang="en"><img class="brand-signature" src="/assets/brand-v4/project-odisha-en-colour.svg" alt="Project Odisha" width="850" height="140"></span></a><nav class="utk-nav desktop-navigation" aria-label="Main navigation" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><a href="/explore/" aria-current="page" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Explore</a><a href="/books/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Books</a><a href="/journey/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>My journey</a><a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Our purpose</a><a class="nav-contribute utk-button utk-button--secondary" href="/contribute/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Be part of it <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>↗</span></a></nav><details class="mobile-navigation" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><summary data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Menu</summary><nav aria-label="Main navigation" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g><a href="/explore/" aria-current="page" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Explore</a><a href="/books/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Books</a><a href="/journey/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>My journey</a><a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Our purpose</a><a href="/contribute/" data-astro-cid-fzpbxy5g>Be part of it</a></nav></details></header><script type="module">var e=document.querySelector(`.mobile-navigation`);e?.addEventListener(`keydown`,t=>{t.key===`Escape`&&e.open&&(e.open=!1,e.querySelector(`summary`)?.focus(),t.preventDefault())});</script><main id="main" tabindex="-1"><article class="article-reading" data-astro-cid-abn45z7c><header data-astro-cid-abn45z7c><h1 data-astro-cid-abn45z7c>Binchana and the Handmade Breeze</h1><p class="article-byline" data-astro-cid-6jw2r4la>By Ahimanikya Satapathy</p></header><div data-astro-cid-abn45z7c><p>With a hand fan, a breeze is something you make. In Odisha, the binchana takes different forms. Subhashish Panigrahi’s photograph shows one decorated cloth example, with flowers, reflective centres and a bright border.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-1" id="user-content-fnref-1" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">1</a></sup></p>
</div><figure class="binchana-portrait" data-astro-cid-abn45z7c><img src="/images/stories/binchana-from-odisha.webp" srcset="/images/responsive/f4e4108ab5b2cde9-480.webp 480w, /images/responsive/b0fe97485633ac07-960.webp 960w" sizes="(max-width: 518px) calc(100vw - 2.4rem), 480px" decoding="async" alt="A decorated hand fan with a dark face, red and yellow edging, floral patterns and small reflective centres." width="960" height="1163" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" data-astro-cid-abn45z7c="true"><figcaption data-astro-cid-abn45z7c>A decorated Binchana photographed in Odisha.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-1" id="user-content-fnref-1-2" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">1</a></sup></figcaption></figure><div data-astro-cid-abn45z7c>
<h2 id="leaf-and-bamboo">Leaf and bamboo</h2>
<p>Palm leaf offers another starting point. Sudam C. Sahu and Ankita Das’s 2024 study of plant-based artifacts draws on interviews with Odisha artisans during 2019–21. Its supplementary table records tala leaves, from <em>Borassus flabellifer</em>, among the materials used for hand fans, with Cuttack and Puri listed as locations.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-2" id="user-content-fnref-2" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">2</a></sup></p>
<p>In Purnachandrapur, Baripada, bamboo artisans made binchana alongside baskets. A 2 November 2017 report lists their products, distinguishing the hand fan from the kula used for winnowing.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-3" id="user-content-fnref-3" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">3</a></sup></p>
<p>Palm-leaf fans still had rural buyers when Shantilata Behera of Patana spoke to a reporter in 2018. The Jagatsinghpur account describes rising raw-material costs and demand for fans during power cuts.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-3" id="user-content-fnref-3-2" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">3</a></sup> The difficulty lay partly in obtaining the leaves from which useful objects could be made.</p>
<h2 id="cloth-and-occasion">Cloth and occasion</h2>
<p>A separate museum example gives a fan another setting. The Virtual Museum of Images and Sounds catalogues an Alata in the Kala Bhoomi Odisha Crafts Museum collection, on loan from SIDAC Show House. Its description places the object within appliqué work, where cloth designs are stitched to a base cloth that may be mounted on a frame. The entry associates alata with fanning kings and deities.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-4" id="user-content-fnref-4" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">4</a></sup></p>
<p>Panigrahi’s photographed cloth fan is a separate example; its maker and intended occasion are not identified.<sup><a href="#user-content-fn-1" id="user-content-fnref-1-3" data-footnote-ref aria-describedby="footnote-label">1</a></sup></p>
<p>Leaf, bamboo and stitched cloth give the hand fan different surfaces and forms. Looking closely at a binchana means noticing the choices that let an object move air—and sometimes carry a pattern or serve an occasion as well.</p>
<section data-footnotes class="article-footnotes"><h2 id="footnote-label">Footnotes &amp; Sources</h2>
<ol>
<li id="user-content-fn-1">
<p>Subhashish Panigrahi, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Binchana_from_Odisha.jpg">“Binchana from Odisha”</a>, photograph dated 15 July 2013, Wikimedia Commons. <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>. Web version resized and compressed. <a href="#user-content-fnref-1" data-footnote-backref="" aria-label="Back to reference 1" class="data-footnote-backref">↩</a> <a href="#user-content-fnref-1-2" data-footnote-backref="" aria-label="Back to reference 1-2" class="data-footnote-backref">↩<sup>2</sup></a> <a href="#user-content-fnref-1-3" data-footnote-backref="" aria-label="Back to reference 1-3" class="data-footnote-backref">↩<sup>3</sup></a></p>
</li>
